    "6.1": `<p>Acidity is governed by the free energy of the conjugate base relative to the acid, so any factor that stabilizes {{A-}} (induction, size, hybridization, aromaticity, resonance, electronegativity) lowers the {{pKa}} of H-A.</p>`,
    "6.2": `<p>The lecture's rule for rationalizing {{pKa}} values is to <b>look at the conjugate base</b>. Because a substituent affects a charged base much more than the neutral acid, differences in acidity are mostly differences in the stability of the base {{A-}}: whatever stabilizes {{A-}} makes the acid H-A stronger.</p>
<p>The first example compares an ordinary carboxylic acid, {{pKa}} 4, with trifluoroacetic acid, {{pKa}} −1.5:</p>
[[mol:CC(=O)O]]
[[mol:OC(=O)C(F)(F)F]]
<p>The conjugate base {{CF3CO2-}} is <b>stabilized by induction</b>. The three fluorine atoms are strongly electronegative and polarize the sigma framework, pulling electron density toward themselves. That withdraws some of the negative charge from the carboxylate oxygens and spreads it over more of the ion. Part of the effect also acts through space, as a field effect. A more stable base means a stronger acid.</p>
<p>The lecture then lists the <b>factors that stabilize a base</b>:</p>
<table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>How it stabilizes the base</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td><b>Induction</b></td><td>nearby electronegative atoms pull charge through bonds</td><td>{{CF3CO2H}} −1.5, {{R-CO2H}} 4</td></tr><tr><td><b>Size</b></td><td>a larger atom spreads the charge out</td><td>{{H2S}} 10, {{H2O}} 15.7</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hybridization</b></td><td>more s character holds the pair closer to the nucleus</td><td>H-C≡C-H 25, {{CH3CH3}} 60</td></tr><tr><td><b>Aromaticity</b></td><td>the base is an aromatic ion</td><td>cyclopentadiene, about 16</td></tr><tr><td><b>Resonance</b></td><td>the charge is shared by several atoms</td><td>{{R-CO2H}} 4, R-OH 16</td></tr><tr><td><b>Electronegativity</b></td><td>a more electronegative atom holds the charge better</td><td>{{H2O}} 15.7, {{NH3}} 38, {{CH4}} 50 to 60</td></tr></table>
<p>The lecture works through induction, hybridization and size in detail. Water and ammonia ({{pKa}} 15.7 against 38) differ chiefly in electronegativity, since oxygen accommodates a negative charge better than nitrogen.</p>`,
    "6.3": `<p>The lecture's rule for rationalizing {{pKa}} values is to <b>look at the conjugate base</b>. Because a substituent affects a charged base much more than the neutral acid, differences in acidity are mostly differences in the stability of the base {{A-}}: whatever stabilizes {{A-}} makes the acid H-A stronger.</p>
<p>The first example compares an ordinary carboxylic acid, {{pKa}} 4, with trifluoroacetic acid, {{pKa}} −1.5:</p>
[[mol:CC(=O)O]]
[[mol:OC(=O)C(F)(F)F]]
<p>The conjugate base {{CF3CO2-}} is <b>stabilized by induction</b>. The three fluorine atoms are strongly electronegative and polarize the sigma framework, pulling electron density toward themselves. That withdraws some of the negative charge from the carboxylate oxygens and spreads it over more of the ion. Part of the effect also acts through space, as a field effect. A more stable base means a stronger acid.</p>
<p>The lecture then lists the <b>factors that stabilize a base</b>:</p>
<table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>How it stabilizes the base</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td><b>Induction</b></td><td>nearby electronegative atoms pull charge through bonds</td><td>{{CF3CO2H}} −1.5, {{R-CO2H}} 4</td></tr><tr><td><b>Size</b></td><td>a larger atom spreads the charge out</td><td>{{H2S}} 10, {{H2O}} 15.7</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hybridization</b></td><td>more s character holds the pair closer to the nucleus</td><td>H-C≡C-H 25, {{CH3CH3}} 60</td></tr><tr><td><b>Aromaticity</b></td><td>the base is an aromatic ion</td><td>cyclopentadiene, about 16</td></tr><tr><td><b>Resonance</b></td><td>the charge is shared by several atoms</td><td>{{R-CO2H}} 4, R-OH 16</td></tr><tr><td><b>Electronegativity</b></td><td>a more electronegative atom holds the charge better</td><td>{{H2O}} 15.7, {{NH3}} 38, {{CH4}} 50 to 60</td></tr></table>
<p>The lecture works through induction, hybridization and size in detail. Water and ammonia ({{pKa}} 15.7 against 38) differ chiefly in electronegativity, since oxygen accommodates a negative charge better than nitrogen. For hybridization, an sp carbon has one half s character, against one quarter for an {{sp3}} carbon, so the lone pair of the acetylide lies closer to the nucleus and is held more tightly. For aromaticity, the anion of cyclopentadiene is aromatic, and that is why this hydrocarbon has a {{pKa}} of about 16.</p>
<p>The inductive effect here can be put in numbers. The two acids differ by 4 − (−1.5) = 5.5 {{pKa}} units, so {{CF3CO2H}} is 10<sup>5.5</sup>, about 300,000 times, stronger than the ordinary acid. Since {{pKa}} is proportional to the standard free energy of dissociation, each unit is worth 1.36 kcal/mol at 25 °C (RT × 2.303, with RT = 0.592), and the three fluorines stabilize the conjugate base by about 5.5 × 1.36 = 7.5 kcal/mol relative to its acid. Induction operates through sigma bonds, with a field effect through space, and it fades quickly with distance: it is strongest when the electronegative atoms are on the carbon next to the charged group, and it is additive, so three fluorines do more than one.</p>
<p>The standard error is to explain the acid when the base should be explained. A weak answer says that the fluorines weaken the O-H bond or make the hydrogen more positive. The reasoning the lecture wants, and the reasoning that earns the points, compares the two <b>conjugate bases</b>. Write both anions, say which is more stable and name the factor: {{CF3CO2-}} is more stable than {{CH3CO2-}} because of induction, so {{CF3CO2H}} is the stronger acid. The same three-part sentence (draw the bases, pick the more stable, name the factor) works for every acidity comparison in this course.</p>
<p>The basis of the method is thermodynamic. Acidity is the position of the equilibrium between H-A and its ions. Lowering the energy of the conjugate base lowers the energy of the product side, so the equilibrium shifts toward dissociation and {{Ka}} increases. Strictly, the free energy of dissociation depends on both the acid and its base, but a substituent changes the energy of the charged base much more than that of the neutral acid, so the base accounts for most of the difference. The acid has not changed: its base has become a better place for the electrons to be.</p>
<p>Resonance was not worked through in this lecture, but it is on the list and the past exam leans on it. A carboxylic acid (4) is 12 units stronger than an alcohol (16) because the carboxylate delocalizes its charge equally over two oxygens. That is why acetate is a weak base, which is the key to Question 7A: with 2-bromopropane, acetate substitutes while the much stronger base ethoxide eliminates. Phenol's {{pKa}} of 10 in Question 1A has the same cause, a charge delocalized into the ring. Resonance also makes tosylate a good leaving group in Question 2A.</p>`,
